00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:06.720
Hello! In today's Bug Bounty Report Explained
you will learn how it was possible to exploit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:14.240
blind SSRF vulnerability in Google Cloud. Blind&nbsp;
SSRFs often have little to no impact but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:20.640
time the researcher was able to exploit it&nbsp;
successfully and he got $31,000 for it. Link&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:27.200
to his original blogpost is in the description.&nbsp;
Also, I've recreated the vulnerability and created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.480
hands-on labs. You can try them for free using the&nbsp;
link in the description. You only have to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:39.760
your email. But for now, enjoy the video! Google&nbsp;
cloud monitoring is a service that allows you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:46.160
see metrics for your Google Cloud infrastructure.&nbsp;
And Uptime Checks is a functionality that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.800
send a request to your server to check if it's&nbsp;
okay and potentially notify if your server is&nbsp;down. 

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.760
Basic settings allow you to choose the&nbsp;
protocol, hostname and path of your server's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:06.160
endpoint. So this sounds like a perfect&nbsp;
situation to look for SSRF vulnerability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.760
And instances that perform those uptime checks&nbsp;
as well work on Google Cloud infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.880
and as all other virtual machines&nbsp;
they have metadata endpoints on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:25.680
internal IPs. If we could read a response&nbsp;
from this endpoint using SSRF vulnerability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.320
we could, for example, steal service account tokens.&nbsp;
So impact of such vulnerability would be critical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:41.040
But to protect against SSRFs those endpoints&nbsp;
require a specific header. This can successfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:47.280
prevent attacks when the attacker can only&nbsp;
control the URL of the request, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:55.040
won't prevent SSRFs here it's because the uptime&nbsp;
checks feature allows user to set custom headers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:04.400
So the researcher tried to request this endpoint&nbsp;
and he saw 200 OK response within 2 milliseconds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:10.480
It was a concrete evidence that the server&nbsp;
indeed made the request to the internal IP.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:18.000
But so far it's only a blind SSRF and&nbsp;
we are not able to show any impact here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.320
The question is if we can&nbsp;
somehow read the response.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:28.720
And here, another advanced feature of uptime&nbsp;
checks comes into play - response validation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:35.680
You can write a string that you expect to be&nbsp;
present in the response. This could be useful, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:43.520
example, in a situation where your server returns&nbsp;
always 200 response code even for 404 responses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.560
So the easiest way of exfiltrating data&nbsp;
using such functionality looks like this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.960
we have a part of the response that we know. In&nbsp;
this case, let's assume it's a beginning of JSON&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:03.520
that looks like this. And when you tell the uptime&nbsp;
text feature that you expect this to be present in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:10.480
your response it's always true because it's always&nbsp;
in the response. But what if we try to search for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:18.480
such string with "a" as the first letter of the&nbsp;
actual access token? If the uptime check fails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:25.680
it means that the access token doesn't start&nbsp;
with "a" letter. So then you try with letter "b",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:33.920
"c", "d" and so on and finally you hit a match,&nbsp;
let's say with letter "e". So now your known part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:41.040
of the response is one character bigger. So now&nbsp;
you try the same with the access token that starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:48.880
with "ea", "eb", "ec" and so on and so forth.&nbsp;
You go over and over again until you exfiltrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:56.000
the whole token. That's basically how blind&nbsp;
sql injection is being exploited. This way the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.640
researcher was able to exfiltrate some data but&nbsp;
unfortunately not all of it. Because there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.480
a small problem - uptime checks are performed&nbsp;
from different instances all over the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.800
There were about 54 of them and&nbsp;
each of them has unique access token.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:22.960
So if your uptime check fails you don't know if&nbsp;
you just tried incorrect character or if you just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:32.000
hit another instance. In fact, with 54 instances&nbsp;
the chance of hitting another instance is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:39.120
much higher than hitting the one you want to hit.&nbsp;
The researcher stopped here, submitted the report&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:46.960
and got the reward of $31.000. The next part of&nbsp;
the video is based on the comment from Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:54.000
itself who tried to escalate the issue and created&nbsp;
a proof of concept that was actually able to steal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:03.120
the access token. One idea is to send 54 requests.&nbsp;
Then, statistically you would hit your instance at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:09.840
least once. But in practice you wouldn't have the&nbsp;
guarantee and with multiple characters this surely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:16.960
wouldn't work well. To solve this problem you can&nbsp;
use positive and negative regular expressions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:24.720
So in previously mentioned situation you just&nbsp;
sent two filters. Square brackets in regexes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:34.400
indicate a range of characters. For example, [a-z]&nbsp;
indicates all lowercase letters. A caret means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:42.720
negative search so [^a-z] matches everything but&nbsp;
a lowercase letter. In this example, we are only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.080
using the "a" character so we don't really need&nbsp;
the square brackets but we will need them in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:54.240
second. In such case, when you hit a positive&nbsp;
regex it's awesome you have the next character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.960
of the token. In case you don't have the&nbsp;
positive match but you have the negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:05.840
it means that this character is not the next in&nbsp;
the token and you have to try "b", "c" and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:12.240
If you get neither of those matches you need to&nbsp;
send the same request again, because it means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:19.440
that you hit the wrong instance. At the beginning&nbsp;
when you only have a few characters of the token&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.680
it's possible that tokens from different machines&nbsp;
will have collisions, but we don't care about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:31.427
because we don't target a specific instance but&nbsp;
we want to leak any token. 

00:06:31.427 --> 00:06:41.760
According to Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;after about 10 characters the token gets unique.&nbsp;
And statistically you would need 54 requests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:48.400
to hit the correct instance. This means you would&nbsp;
need to send thousands of requests to reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:55.280
the whole token. And those access tokens are&nbsp;
only valid for 60 minutes. So with rate limiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:02.080
it's likely that the token would be expired before&nbsp;
you are able to leak it to the end. But we can use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:08.400
regular expressions to speed-up this process&nbsp;
significantly. First, we can brute force not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:15.680
only the next character but we can brute force the&nbsp;
whole token using wildcards. The dot stands for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:23.360
any character in regexes so you can use multiple&nbsp;
dots to brute force all characters in parallel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:30.240
This reduces the time of requests significantly.&nbsp;
Another useful trick is using binary search.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:37.520
We don't need to check each character one by one&nbsp;
like first "a", then "b", then "c" and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:43.840
Using regexes we can specify ranges of&nbsp;
characters. So we can implement binary search.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:50.640
First, we can send such request to determine&nbsp;
if the next character is a lowercase letter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:58.160
uppercase letter, a digit or any character that&nbsp;
can be inside the token. Let's assume it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:05.280
lowercase letter. The next step is verifying if&nbsp;
the character is within the first or the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:12.960
half of the alphabet. Then, you divide that into&nbsp;
further halves until you get to a single letter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.720
This way you also reduce the number&nbsp;
of requests that you have to send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:25.440
to determine each character. Of course, the&nbsp;
final exploit must have been really complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:31.280
because you have to combine all those three&nbsp;
techniques at once. So positive-negative regexes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:36.560
binary search and using wildcards to&nbsp;
probe multiple characters at once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:45.360
This way the exploit was able to leak the access&nbsp;
token within 15 minutes, including rate limiting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

